<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:db="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:scilab="http://www.scilab.org" xml:lang="en" xml:id="linkage">
    <refnamediv>
        <refname>linkage</refname>
        <refpurpose>Agglomerative hierarchical clustering</refpurpose>
    </refnamediv>
    <refsynopsisdiv>
        <title>Calling Sequence</title>
        <synopsis>
            z = linkage(x)
            z = linkage(x, method)
            z = linkage(x, method, metric)
        </synopsis>
    </refsynopsisdiv>
    <refsection role="arguments">
        <title>Arguments</title>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>x</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>A double observation matrix or a condensed distance vector.</para>
                    <para>If <literal>x</literal> is a matrix, each row represents an observation. 
                        Distances are computed with <link linkend="pdist">pdist</link> 
                        and then transformed with <link linkend="squareform">squareform</link>.</para>
                    <para>If <literal>x</literal> is a vector, it is interpreted as a condensed distance vector.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>method</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Linkage method. The default value is <literal>"single"</literal>.</para>
                    <itemizedlist>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"single"</literal>: single linkage.</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"complete"</literal>: complete linkage.</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"average"</literal>: average linkage unweighted by cluster sizes (UPGMA).</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"weighted"</literal>: weighted average linkage (WPGMA).</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"centroid"</literal>: centroid method (UPGMC).</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"median"</literal>: median method (WPGMC).</para></listitem>
                        <listitem><para><literal>"ward"</literal>: Ward method.</para></listitem>
                    </itemizedlist>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>metric</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Metric used by <link linkend="pdist">pdist</link> when <literal>x</literal> is a matrix. 
                        Default value: <literal>"euclidean"</literal>.</para>
                    <para>A cell can be used to pass additional arguments to <literal>pdist</literal>, 
                        for example, to set the Minkowski metric.</para>
                    <para>The methods <literal>"centroid"</literal>, <literal>"median"</literal> et <literal>"ward"</literal> 
                        require the Euclidean metric.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>z</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>A <literal>(n-1) x 3</literal> matrix.</para>
                    <para>Each row contains the identifiers of the two merged clusters and the corresponding distance.</para>
                    <para>Initial observations are identified from <literal>1</literal> to <literal>n</literal>. 
                        The new clusters are numbered starting from <literal>n+1</literal>.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
    </refsection>
    <refsection role="description">
        <title>Description</title>
        <para><literal>linkage</literal> performs agglomerative hierarchical clustering.</para>
        <para>Each observation initially constitutes a cluster. At each step, the two closest clusters are merged, and the distances are updated according to the chosen method.</para>
        <para>For <literal>n</literal> observations, <literal>linkage</literal> performs <literal>n-1</literal> merges and returns a matrix <literal>(n-1) x 3</literal>.</para>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Methods</title>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>single</term>
                <listitem><para><literal>d(U,k) = min(d(r,k), d(c,k))</literal></para></listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>complete</term>
                <listitem><para><literal>d(U,k) = max(d(r,k), d(c,k))</literal></para></listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>average</term>
                <listitem><para><literal>d(U,k) = (nr*d(r,k) + nc*d(c,k)) / (nr+nc)</literal></para></listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>weighted</term>
                <listitem><para><literal>d(U,k) = (d(r,k) + d(c,k)) / 2</literal></para></listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>centroid</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para><literal>d(U,k)^2 = nr/(nr+nc)*d(r,k)^2 + nc/(nr+nc)*d(c,k)^2 - nr*nc/(nr+nc)^2*d(r,c)^2</literal></para>
                    <para>Requires the Euclidean metric.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>median</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para><literal>d(U,k)^2 = 1/2*d(r,k)^2 + 1/2*d(c,k)^2 - 1/4*d(r,c)^2</literal></para>
                    <para>Requires the Euclidean metric.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>ward</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para><literal>d(U,k)^2 = ((nr+nk)*d(r,k)^2 + (nc+nk)*d(c,k)^2 - nk*d(r,c)^2) / (nr+nc+nk)</literal></para>
                    <para>Requires the Euclidean metric.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
        <para>
            Where:
        </para>
        <itemizedlist>
            <listitem><para><literal>r</literal>: index of the first cluster being merged.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>c</literal>: index of the second cluster being merged.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>U</literal>: the new cluster created by merging r and c.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>k</literal>: index of any other active cluster, different from r and c.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>nr</literal>: number of original observations contained in cluster r.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>nc</literal>: number of original observations contained in cluster c.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>nk</literal>: number of original observations contained in cluster k.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>d(r,k)</literal>: distance between clusters r and k.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>d(c,k)</literal>: distance between clusters c and k.</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>d(r,c)</literal>: distance between the two clusters being merged..</para></listitem>
            <listitem><para><literal>d(U,k)</literal>: distance between the newly created cluster U and cluster k.</para></listitem>
        </itemizedlist>
    </refsection>
    <refsection role="example">
    <title>Examples</title>
        <para>
            <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
            X = [2  8  0
                1  9  9
                3  4  2
                8  1  6
                5  5  5
                7  2  1];

            Z = linkage(X)
            ]]></programlisting>
            <screen><![CDATA[ 
--> X = [2  8  0
    1  9  9
    3  4  2
    8  1  6
    5  5  5
    7  2  1];

--> Z = linkage(X)
Z = [5x3 double]

   3.   5.    3.7416574
   1.   7.    4.5825757
   6.   8.    4.5825757
   4.   9.    5.0990195
   2.   10.   6.9282032
   ]]></screen>
        </para>
        <para>
            <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
                X = [2  8  0
                1  9  9
                3  4  2
                8  1  6
                5  5  5
                7  2  1];
                methods = ["single", "complete", "average", "weighted", "centroid", "median", "ward"];

                for method = methods
                    Z = linkage(X, method);
                    disp(method + ":", Z);
                end
            ]]></programlisting>
        </para>
        <para>
            <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
                X = [2  8  0
                    1  9  9
                    3  4  2
                    8  1  6
                    5  5  5
                    7  2  1];
                Z = linkage(X, "average", "cityblock");
            ]]></programlisting>
            <screen><![CDATA[ 
--> X = [2  8  0
    1  9  9
    3  4  2
    8  1  6
    5  5  5
    7  2  1];

--> Z = linkage(X, "average", "cityblock")
 Z = [5x3 double]

   3.   5.    6.       
   4.   6.    7.       
   1.   7.    9.       
   8.   9.    11.166667
   2.   10.   15.2
   ]]></screen>
        </para>
        <para>
            <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
                X = [2  8  0
                    1  9  9
                    3  4  2
                    8  1  6
                    5  5  5
                    7  2  1];
                p = 3;
                Z = linkage(X, "average", {"minkowski", p})
            ]]></programlisting>
        
            <screen><![CDATA[ 
--> X = [2  8  0
    1  9  9
    3  4  2
    8  1  6
    5  5  5
    7  2  1];

--> p = 3;
--> Z = linkage(X, "average", {"minkowski", p})
 Z = [5x3 double]

   3.   5.    3.3019272
   6.   7.    4.4027021
   4.   8.    5.1802944
   1.   9.    6.4984308
   2.   10.   8.4813034
            ]]></screen>
        </para>
        <para>
            <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
                Y = [3 4 10 12 5 11 13 6 8 2];
                Z = linkage(Y, "average")
            ]]></programlisting>
            <screen><![CDATA[ 
--> Y = [3 4 10 12 5 11 13 6 8 2];
--> Z = linkage(Y, "average")
 Z = [4x3 double]

   4.   5.   2. 
   1.   2.   3. 
   3.   7.   4.5
   6.   8.   10.
            ]]></screen>
        </para>
    </refsection>
